{"kind":"report","text":"Exact-head review of b859070bca08892050ca5e1381e3218526a7fb3a, resting on all 31 named artifacts, primary notes/2026-10-01-laneG-checkers.md first. Changes requested.\n\nArchitecture: the separate publisher and runner, external signing key, argument-array commands, exact HEAD/tree/snapshot confirmations, global inputs, and machine-labelled checks are sound foundations. The filtered snapshot is a root commit with only selected objects; I reviewed the five Git changes against approved lane B f57b6c8 and all new lock descriptors. The actual service entrypoints still submit to HarnessLedger, as the report says; the real Room binding and job authentication contract remain explicit integration work, not demonstrated fulfillment of real Room obligations. This review does not waive them or the proposed amendment 3 gaps.\n\nSecurity:\nG1 [P1] Whole-tree jobs reuse a writable, previously untrusted container (worker.ts runners; container.ts:107-114, 142-149). close deletes only /work/\u003cjob\u003e; it does not restore the image filesystem or stop surviving processes. Repository npm scripts can alter shared tools/files outside that directory, while subsequent jobs trust those tools for checkout, execution and the cached runner digest. Independent local proof used the actual RunnerBox/TestsChecker with a disposable model of the container's shared writable tool directory: a known failing integration failed, another job installed an npm executable that exits 0, that job's directory was removed, then the SAME failing integration/tree received a signed passing check with the SAME runner digest. No host tools or live service were changed. Prevent all cross-job filesystem/process contamination; reuse only a clean trusted image/cache beneath fresh per-job isolation, or destroy the untrusted instance each time. Add an adversarial previous-job/next-job regression, including surviving processes and trusted tools.\n\nG2 [P1] Concurrent whole-tree jobs of one room/checker share one RunnerBox and one container-wide outbound grant (worker.ts:open; container.ts:100-114). A second open replaces the first job's gateway with its token/repository; requests contain no originating job identity, and the first close revokes the second job's access. Independent test of actual RunnerBox/RunnerGateway: open A then B; A's repo is denied, traffic from the still-running shared container reads B's private response with token B, and close A denies B. Give each active job an isolated runner/grant, or enforce exclusive ownership across the ENTIRE open→all execs→close lifetime, with an owner token so old closes cannot revoke newer grants. Cover overlap, same-ID retry and error/cleanup ordering. Directory names alone do not provide isolation.\n\nG3 [P2] The checker borrows the mutable caller job throughout asynchronous execution (job.ts checkJob returns the original; checker.ts:124-171). After checkout has verified commit A, a caller can change integration/config/input/target while npm test is awaiting; the service then signs those later fields for the result of A. Independent real-git/npm test mutated integration and config only after checkout, while the test step waited; the signed passing envelope bound the substituted integration/config and verified cryptographically. Take an owned deep snapshot before the first await, validate and use only it for opening, checkout, execution, signing and after-hooks. Test mutation of nested input paths and all binding fields, not just the outer object.\n\nScalability:\nG4 [P2] RunnerBox.exec silently redacts and tails EVERY stdout at 65,536 characters (container.ts:132), including machine-readable git ls-tree used for snapshot verification (runner.ts). A valid scoped project with 1,800 small source files was built with the real snapshot publisher and then failed verification through the actual RunnerBox output path, solely because ls-tree was truncated. Path names matching the token regex are also altered before the digest is computed. Preserve complete, unmodified structured Git output for verification, or stream it through an explicit safe bounded interface; truncate/redact human detail separately. Test a scoped tree beyond 64 KiB and a legal credential-shaped filename, and make genuine resource-limit refusals explicit rather than manufacturing a failed check.\n\nSimplification: make the job and session ownership boundaries explicit and reusable for all three checkers; keep harness and production submission separate; keep display clipping separate from structured execution results. Correct the README's claim that separate job directories share nothing.\n\nValidation: npm ci installed 211 packages; root type check and the clean checkers package type check passed; 13 candidate checker tests, 115 Git Node tests and 8 Git workerd tests passed; four independent diagnostics passed while asserting the above incorrect behaviors. Read both supplied live result files and their driver; the documented pass/fail, changed-test rerun, excluded-file, push-denial and dated cold/warm observations are supported. Those sequential benign live cases do not establish adversarial job isolation. Diagnostic files were removed from the candidate tree after execution so they do not contaminate its type check; retained under /tmp/artroom-checker-G-diagnostics. No fresh live deployment or mutation was performed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:6b96818c60e890c59b8a051778dcf90e9e8e70e8","binding":"assigned","head":"b859070bca08892050ca5e1381e3218526a7fb3a","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f6fc2e46e3cbe467f9aa155a9d5300a6be485abf\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:242988aa57fe346d38f90f5e629a6b96b5cd7c6e\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b84fbd41f42360fc1fc5dea2c976410e7497a150","review_path":"reviewgua